<section xml:id="sec-command-window">
	<title>Command Window</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			Software applications require a way for users to interact with them. This interaction occurs through a <term>User Interface</term> (UI), which is typically either a command-line or graphical interface:
			<dl>
				<li>
					<title>Command Line Interface (CLI)</title>
					<p>
						Users type commands at a prompt to control the application. MATLAB calls this the Command Window.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<title>Graphical User Interface (GUI)</title>
					<p>
						Users interact through visual elements such as buttons, menus, and windows.
					</p>
				</li>
			</dl>
		</p>

		<p>
			MATLAB is unusual in that it provides a CLI directly inside its GUI. This embedded CLI is the <xref ref="command-window" text="custom">Command Window</xref>. You type commands at the <pf>&gt;&gt;</pf> prompt and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to evaluate them.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-command-window-structure">
		<title>🧱 Structure</title>

		<assemblage xml:id="command-window-structure">
			<title>Using the Command Window</title>

			<p>
				The basic pattern for using the Command Window is:
			</p>

			<program language="matlab">
				>> expression_or_command
			</program>

			<p>
				Key rules:
				<ul>
					<li>Type a mathematical expression or command at the <pf>&gt;&gt;</pf> prompt and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
					<li>If you do not assign the result to a variable, MATLAB stores it in the special variable <pf>ans</pf>.</li>
					<li>End a command with a <term>semicolon</term> <pf>;</pf> to suppress output display; the result is still computed and stored.</li>
					<li>MATLAB includes built-in constants such as <pf>pi</pf>, and functions such as <pf>sin</pf>, <pf>cos</pf>, <pf>sqrt</pf>, and <pf>abs</pf>. Euler’s number can be obtained with <pf>exp(1)</pf>.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				Session management commands:
				<ul>
					<li><pf>clear</pf> — removes all variables from the workspace.</li>
					<li><pf>clc</pf> — clears the Command Window display (does not delete variables).</li>
					<li><pf>format</pf> — controls how numerical output is displayed (not how it is stored).</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</assemblage>

		<exercise label="cmdwin-reading-ckpt-1">

			<task label="cmdwin-reading-ckpt-1a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						When you enter an expression in the Command Window without assigning it to a variable, how does MATLAB store the result?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p>It stores the result in the special variable <pf>ans</pf>.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB always stores the last unassigned result in <pf>ans</pf>, which appears in the Workspace.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>It permanently saves the result to a file.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Results are stored in memory (the workspace), not automatically saved to a file.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>It discards the result immediately.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>MATLAB always stores the result; unassigned results go into <pf>ans</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p>It replaces the entire contents of the workspace.</p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Only the value of <pf>ans</pf> is updated. Other variables in the workspace are not affected.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-reading-ckpt-1b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Which command clears variables from memory without closing MATLAB?
					</p>
				</statement>
				<choices randomize="yes">
					<choice correct="yes">
						<statement><p><pf>clear</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p>Correct. <pf>clear</pf> removes all variables from the workspace without closing MATLAB or clearing the Command Window display.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>clc</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p><pf>clc</pf> clears the Command Window display but does not remove variables from memory.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>format</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p><pf>format</pf> controls how numbers are displayed, not memory management.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
					<choice>
						<statement><p><pf>exit</pf></p></statement>
						<feedback><p><pf>exit</pf> closes MATLAB entirely. To clear just variables, use <pf>clear</pf>.</p></feedback>
					</choice>
				</choices>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-reading-ckpt-1c">
				<statement correct="no">
					<p>
						Adding a semicolon at the end of a MATLAB command prevents the variable from being created.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<feedback>
					<p>
						Incorrect. A semicolon only suppresses the output display in the Command Window. The variable is still created and stored in the workspace.
					</p>
				</feedback>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-command-window-examples">
		<title>👀 Examples</title>

		<p>
			Here are concrete examples of using the Command Window as an interactive calculator.
		</p>

		<example xml:id="ex-command-window-arithmetic">
			<title>Arithmetic in the Command Window</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					You can use the Command Window like a scientific calculator. MATLAB evaluates the expression and displays the result:
				</p>

				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
					<stack>
						<p>Commands ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							2 + 3
							10 / 4
							2^8
							sqrt(144)
						</program>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>Output ⤵︎</p>
						<pre>
ans =
     5

ans =
    2.5000

ans =
   256

ans =
    12
						</pre>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>

				<p>
					Each result is stored in <pf>ans</pf> and displayed. Notice that <pf>ans</pf> is overwritten each time a new unassigned expression is evaluated.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<example xml:id="ex-command-window-builtin">
			<title>Built-in Constants and Functions</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					MATLAB includes many built-in mathematical constants and functions. Here are some common ones:
				</p>

				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
					<stack>
						<p>Commands ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							pi
							sin(pi/6)
							abs(-7.5)
							round(3.7)
						</program>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>Output ⤵︎</p>
						<pre>
ans =
    3.1416

ans =
    0.5000

ans =
    7.5000

ans =
     4
						</pre>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>

				<p>
					MATLAB displays <pf>pi</pf> rounded to 4 decimal places by default, but internally stores the full double-precision value.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<example xml:id="ex-command-window-session-mgmt">
			<title>Managing Your Session</title>

			<statement>
				<p>
					As you work, the Command Window fills up with output and the Workspace accumulates variables. Use these commands to stay organized:
				</p>

				<sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
					<stack>
						<p>Commands ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							x = 5;
							y = 10;
							clear
							clc
							format long
							pi
						</program>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>What happens ⤵︎</p>
						<program language="matlab">
							% x created silently (semicolon)
							% y created silently (semicolon)
							% workspace cleared: x and y removed
							% Command Window display cleared
							% switch to long display format
							% ans = 3.141592653589793
						</program>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>

				<p>
					Note that <pf>clc</pf> only clears the display — it does not remove variables. And <pf>clear</pf> removes variables but does not clear the display.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<exercise label="cmdwin-examples-practice">
			<title>Command Window Practice</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Try these Command Window exercises. Predict the output before evaluating.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="cmdwin-examples-practice-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What will MATLAB display after running:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						3 * 4 + 2
					</program>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<c>ans = 14</c>. MATLAB follows standard order of operations: multiplication before addition. <m>3 \times 4 = 12</m>, then <m>12 + 2 = 14</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-examples-practice-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						What will MATLAB display after running these two lines in sequence?
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						5 + 3
						ans * 2
					</program>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						The first line produces <c>ans = 8</c>. The second line uses <pf>ans</pf> (which is <c>8</c>) and multiplies by 2, producing <c>ans = 16</c>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>
	</subsection>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-command-window-practice-activities">
		<title>🧑🏻‍💻 Practice Activities</title>

		<exercise label="cmdwin-activity-evaluate">
			<title>Evaluate and Predict</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					For each expression below, predict the result, then evaluate it in the Sage cell to confirm.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-evaluate-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Predict the output of:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						2 + 3 * 4
					</program>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							2 + 3 * 4
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <c>ans = 14</c>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<c>ans = 14</c>. Multiplication is performed before addition: <m>3 \times 4 = 12</m>, then <m>2 + 12 = 14</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-evaluate-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Predict the output of:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						(2 + 3) * 4
					</program>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							(2 + 3) * 4
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <c>ans = 20</c>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<c>ans = 20</c>. Parentheses force addition first: <m>2 + 3 = 5</m>, then <m>5 \times 4 = 20</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-evaluate-c">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Predict the output of:
					</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						3^2 + 4^2
					</program>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							3^2 + 4^2
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						When done, evaluate and confirm it prints <c>ans = 25</c>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<c>ans = 25</c>. Exponentiation first: <m>3^2 = 9</m> and <m>4^2 = 16</m>, then <m>9 + 16 = 25</m>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="cmdwin-activity-session-commands">
			<title>Session Management Commands</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Practice using <pf>clear</pf>, <pf>clc</pf>, and <pf>format</pf> to manage your MATLAB session.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-session-a">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Run the following commands and observe what happens after each one:
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% Step 1: Create some variables
							x = 42;
							y = 3.14159;

							% Step 2: Display them
							x
							y

							% Step 3: Try clearing x only
							clear x

							% Step 4: Try to display x (should cause an error)
							% x
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						Uncomment the last line (remove the <pf>%</pf>) and re-evaluate. Observe the error message.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						After <pf>clear x</pf>, trying to use <pf>x</pf> produces an error: <c>error: 'x' undefined</c>. The variable no longer exists in the workspace. However, <pf>y</pf> is unaffected by <pf>clear x</pf>.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-session-b">
				<statement>
					<p>
						Compare the display of <pf>pi</pf> under different <pf>format</pf> settings:
					</p>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							format short
							pi

							format long
							pi
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						Observe how the number of decimal places changes, but the stored value does not change.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<p>
						<pf>format short</pf> displays <c>ans = 3.1416</c> (4 decimal places). <pf>format long</pf> displays <c>ans = 3.141592653589793</c> (15 digits). The internal value of <pf>pi</pf> is the same in both cases.
					</p>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

		<exercise label="cmdwin-activity-builtin-functions">
			<title>Explore Built-in Functions</title>

			<introduction>
				<p>
					Use the Sage cell to evaluate each built-in function. Confirm the result matches the expected output.
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-builtin-a">
				<statement>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							% Evaluate: floor(7.9), ceil(7.1), round(7.5)
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						Confirm: <c>floor(7.9) = 7</c>, <c>ceil(7.1) = 8</c>, <c>round(7.5) = 8</c>.
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						floor(7.9)  % ans = 7
						ceil(7.1)   % ans = 8
						round(7.5)  % ans = 8
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

			<task label="cmdwin-activity-builtin-b">
				<statement>
					<sage language="octave">
						<input>
							% ==== Type your code here ====
							% Evaluate: mod(17, 5)
						</input>
					</sage>
					<p>
						Confirm: <c>ans = 2</c>. (<pf>mod</pf> returns the remainder after division.)
					</p>
				</statement>
				<solution component="example-solution">
					<program language="matlab">
						mod(17, 5)  % ans = 2, because 17 = 3*5 + 2
					</program>
				</solution>
			</task>

		</exercise>

	</subsection>

	<reading-questions xml:id="command-window-reading-questions">
		<title>Reading Questions</title>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-1">
			<statement>
				<p>
					What happens when you type an expression in the Command Window without assigning it to a variable?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>The result is stored in the variable <pf>ans</pf> and displayed.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. MATLAB uses <pf>ans</pf> as a temporary storage for unassigned expressions, and displays the result.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The result is discarded.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The result is not discarded; it is stored in <pf>ans</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB displays an error because no variable was specified.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB does not require you to assign every result. Unassigned results go into <pf>ans</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The result is automatically saved to a file.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Results are stored in memory as <pf>ans</pf>, not automatically saved to a file.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-2">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					Adding a semicolon to the end of a MATLAB command prevents the variable from being stored in the workspace.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					Incorrect. A semicolon suppresses the display in the Command Window, but the variable is still created and stored in the workspace.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-3">
			<statement>
				<p>
					What is the difference between <pf>clear</pf> and <pf>clc</pf>?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><pf>clear</pf> removes variables from the workspace; <pf>clc</pf> clears the Command Window display.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. These are two different cleanup operations. <pf>clear</pf> affects memory; <pf>clc</pf> affects the screen.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><pf>clc</pf> removes variables from the workspace; <pf>clear</pf> clears the Command Window display.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>These are reversed. <pf>clear</pf> removes variables; <pf>clc</pf> clears the display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>Both commands do the same thing: they remove all variables from memory.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>They are not the same. <pf>clear</pf> removes variables; <pf>clc</pf> only clears the visual display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>Both commands clear the Command Window display without affecting variables.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Only <pf>clc</pf> clears the display. <pf>clear</pf> removes variables from memory.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-4">
			<statement correct="yes">
				<p>
					The <pf>format</pf> command changes how numbers are displayed but does not change how they are stored internally.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					Correct. <pf>format</pf> is purely a display setting. The full double-precision value is always stored; only the number of digits shown changes.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-5">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A student runs the following commands:
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					3 + 4
					ans + 10
				</program>
				<p>
					What is displayed after the second command?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p><c>ans = 17</c></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. The first command sets <pf>ans = 7</pf>. The second adds 10 to <pf>ans</pf>, giving 17.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><c>ans = 14</c></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>This would be <m>(3+4)+7</m> = 14, but the second command uses <pf>ans</pf> (which is 7) and adds 10, giving 17.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p><c>ans = 10</c></p></statement>
					<feedback><p>This ignores the first command entirely. MATLAB uses the current value of <pf>ans</pf> (which is 7) and adds 10.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>An error, because <pf>ans</pf> was not explicitly assigned.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB automatically updates <pf>ans</pf> after every unassigned expression, so this is valid.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-6">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which of the following correctly describes what happens when you type <pf>pi</pf> in the Command Window?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>MATLAB displays the value of the built-in constant <pf>pi</pf> and stores it in <pf>ans</pf>.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. <pf>pi</pf> is a built-in constant. Typing it returns its numeric value and stores it in <pf>ans</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB creates a new variable called <pf>pi</pf> with value 0.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB does not create a new variable. It reads the built-in constant and stores the result in <pf>ans</pf>.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB displays the letter <q>pi</q> as text.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB evaluates <pf>pi</pf> as a numeric constant, not as text.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB returns an error because <pf>pi</pf> is not defined.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>pi</pf> is a built-in constant in MATLAB. No error occurs.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-7">
			<statement correct="no">
				<p>
					Running <pf>clc</pf> in the Command Window also removes all variables from the workspace.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					Incorrect. <pf>clc</pf> only clears the Command Window display. Variables remain in the workspace. Use <pf>clear</pf> to remove variables.
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-8">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A student types the following:
				</p>
				<program language="matlab">
					result = 8 * 3;
				</program>
				<p>
					What is true about this command?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is created with value 24, but nothing is displayed in the Command Window.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. The semicolon suppresses output. The variable is created and stored, but the display is silent.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is not created because of the semicolon.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>A semicolon does not prevent variable creation. It only suppresses the display.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>The variable <pf>result</pf> is created and <c>result = 24</c> is displayed.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>The semicolon suppresses the display. The variable is created but not shown.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB displays an error because of the missing output.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>A semicolon is valid MATLAB syntax. No error is produced.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-9">
			<statement correct="yes">
				<p>
					MATLAB is unusual because it provides a command-line interface (CLI) embedded inside a graphical user interface (GUI).
				</p>
			</statement>
			<feedback>
				<p>
					Correct. Most applications use either a CLI or a GUI, but MATLAB provides the Command Window (CLI) inside the MATLAB Desktop (GUI).
				</p>
			</feedback>
		</exercise>

		<exercise label="rq-command-window-10">
			<statement>
				<p>
					After running <pf>format long</pf>, what changes about how MATLAB displays numbers?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<choices randomize="yes">
				<choice correct="yes">
					<statement><p>Numbers are displayed with more decimal places, but the stored values remain the same.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>Correct. <pf>format long</pf> increases display precision to 15 significant digits, but the underlying stored value is unchanged.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>Numbers are stored with more precision.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>MATLAB always stores doubles at full precision. <pf>format</pf> only changes the display, not the stored value.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>All variables are rounded to more decimal places.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p>No rounding occurs. <pf>format long</pf> only changes how many digits are shown on screen.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
				<choice>
					<statement><p>MATLAB switches to a different number type such as integers.</p></statement>
					<feedback><p><pf>format</pf> does not change number types, only the display format.</p></feedback>
				</choice>
			</choices>
		</exercise>
	</reading-questions>

	<exercises xml:id="command-window-coding-exercises">
		<title>Coding Exercises</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<title>Foundational</title>

			<exercise label="cmdwin-ex-basic-calculations">
				<title>Basic Calculations</title>

				<introduction>
					<p>
						Use the Command Window to evaluate the following expressions. Write your MATLAB code and confirm the expected result.
					</p>
				</introduction>

				<task label="cmdwin-ex-basic-a">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Compute the area of a circle with radius <m>r = 7</m> using the formula <m>A = \pi r^2</m>.
						</p>
						<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
							<col width="11%" halign="right" />
							<col width="15%" halign="left" />
							<col width="28%" halign="center" />
							<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
							<row>
								<cell>Inputs:</cell>
								<cell><p><pf>r</pf></p></cell>
								<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
								<cell><p>radius of the circle</p></cell>
							</row>
							<row bottom="minor">
								<cell>Outputs:</cell>
								<cell><p><pf>A</pf></p></cell>
								<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
								<cell><p>area of the circle</p></cell>
							</row>
						</tabular>

						<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
							<stack>
								<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									r = 7;
									A = pi * r^2
								</program>
							</stack>
							<stack>
								<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									A = 153.9380
								</program>
							</stack>
						</sidebyside>
					</statement>
					<solution>
						<program language="matlab">
							r = 7;
							A = pi * r^2
							% A = 153.9380
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>

				<task label="cmdwin-ex-basic-b">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Compute the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs <m>a = 5</m> and <m>b = 12</m> using <m>c = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}</m>.
						</p>

						<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
							<stack>
								<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									a = 5; b = 12;
									c = sqrt(a^2 + b^2)
								</program>
							</stack>
							<stack>
								<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									c = 13
								</program>
							</stack>
						</sidebyside>
					</statement>
					<solution>
						<program language="matlab">
							a = 5;
							b = 12;
							c = sqrt(a^2 + b^2)
							% c = 13
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>

				<task label="cmdwin-ex-basic-c">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Convert a temperature of <m>F = 98.6</m> degrees Fahrenheit to Celsius using <m>C = (F - 32) \cdot 5/9</m>.
						</p>

						<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
							<stack>
								<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									F = 98.6;
									C = (F - 32) * 5/9
								</program>
							</stack>
							<stack>
								<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									C = 37
								</program>
							</stack>
						</sidebyside>
					</statement>
					<solution>
						<program language="matlab">
							F = 98.6;
							C = (F - 32) * 5/9
							% C = 37
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>

			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>

		<exercisegroup>
			<title>Extended</title>

			<exercise label="cmdwin-ex-session-workflow">
				<title>Session Workflow</title>

				<introduction>
					<p>
						These exercises practice using session management commands in a realistic workflow.
					</p>
				</introduction>

				<task label="cmdwin-ex-workflow-a">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Write a sequence of Command Window commands that:
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>Creates a variable <pf>radius</pf> with value <c>5</c> (suppress output with semicolon).</li>
							<li>Creates a variable <pf>area</pf> equal to <m>\pi r^2</m> (suppress output).</li>
							<li>Creates a variable <pf>circumference</pf> equal to <m>2\pi r</m> (suppress output).</li>
							<li>Displays <pf>area</pf> and <pf>circumference</pf> without semicolons.</li>
						</ol>

						<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
							<stack>
								<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									radius = 5;
									area = pi * radius^2;
									circumference = 2 * pi * radius;
									area
									circumference
								</program>
							</stack>
							<stack>
								<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									area = 78.5398
									circumference = 31.4159
								</program>
							</stack>
						</sidebyside>
					</statement>
					<solution>
						<program language="matlab">
							radius = 5;
							area = pi * radius^2;
							circumference = 2 * pi * radius;
							area
							circumference
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>

				<task label="cmdwin-ex-workflow-b">
					<statement>
						<p>
							Starting from a fresh workspace (run <pf>clear</pf> first), write commands to:
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>Compute <m>5!</m> (5 factorial = <m>1 \cdot 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 4 \cdot 5</m>) using MATLAB's built-in <pf>factorial</pf> function.</li>
							<li>Display the result.</li>
							<li>Verify the result using manual multiplication.</li>
						</ol>

						<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
							<stack>
								<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									clear
									result = factorial(5)
									check = 1 * 2 * 3 * 4 * 5
								</program>
							</stack>
							<stack>
								<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
								<program language="matlab">
									result = 120
									check = 120
								</program>
							</stack>
						</sidebyside>
					</statement>
					<solution>
						<program language="matlab">
							clear
							result = factorial(5)
							% result = 120
							check = 1 * 2 * 3 * 4 * 5
							% check = 120
						</program>
					</solution>
				</task>

			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>

	</exercises>

</section>
